@node Debugging
@chapter Debugging

@cindex debugging
@cindex gdb
@cindex breakpoints
@cindex watch variables
@cindex call stack
@cindex User Screen

xwpe integrates with gdb to provide a source-level debugging
environment.  You can set breakpoints, inspect variables, step through
code, view the call stack, and see program output --- all from within
the editor, without screen flicker.

This chapter was verified on 2026-06-01 with xwpe 1.6.3, gdb 17.2,
gcc 15.2, g++ 15.2, gfortran 15.2, fpc 3.2.2, OpenJDK jdb 25,
and Python pdb 3.13.

@section Quick start

@enumerate
@item
Open a source file: @command{wpe debug_test.c} (use @command{wpe},
not @command{we} --- the debugger requires programming mode).

@item
Move to a line inside @code{main()} and press @kbd{Ctrl-G B} to set
a breakpoint.  The line is highlighted.

@item
Press @kbd{Ctrl-G R} to compile, link, and start gdb.  Execution
stops at the breakpoint.

@item
Press @key{F8} to step over lines.  Press @key{F7} to step into
function calls.  The cursor follows execution in the source code.

@item
Press @kbd{Ctrl-G W}, type a variable name, press @key{Return}.
The Watch Window shows its value, updated after each step, or at the
next stop when the program is running.  Watches
work with every debugger: gdb and pdb print them with @code{p}, jdb and
dbx with @code{print}, a68g with @code{evaluate}, a DAP adapter with
its @code{evaluate} request.  A debugger with no such command shows
``watches are not available with @var{debugger}'' on the status line
instead.  A watch the debugger gives no value for (a name the program
does not have) reads @samp{No symbol in current context}.

@item
Press @kbd{Ctrl-G K} to see the call stack.

@item
Press @kbd{Ctrl-G P} to see program output (printf, etc.).
Press any key to return to the editor.

@item
Press @kbd{Ctrl-G Q} to quit the debugger.
@end enumerate

@section Debug menu reference

All commands are available from the @samp{Debug} menu (@kbd{Alt-D})
and via @kbd{Ctrl-G} prefix shortcuts:

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .18 .18 .34
@headitem Command @tab Menu key @tab Shortcut @tab Description
@item Toggle Breakpoint @tab B @tab Ctrl-G B @tab Set/remove breakpoint at cursor
@item Remove all Breakp. @tab M @tab Ctrl-G M @tab Clear all breakpoints
@item Make Watch @tab W @tab Ctrl-G W @tab Add variable to Watch Window
@item Edit watch @tab E @tab Ctrl-G E @tab Edit a watched expression
@item Delete watch @tab D @tab Ctrl-G D @tab Remove a watched variable
@item Remove All watches @tab A @tab Ctrl-G A @tab Clear all watches
@item Show stacK @tab K @tab Ctrl-G K @tab Show call stack
@item Goto cursor @tab U @tab Ctrl-G U @tab Run until cursor line
@item Finish function @tab F @tab Ctrl-G F @tab Run until function returns
@item Trace (step into) @tab T @tab Ctrl-G T @tab Execute one line, enter functions
@item Step (step over) @tab S @tab Ctrl-G S @tab Execute one line, skip functions
@item Run/Continue @tab R @tab Ctrl-G R @tab Start or continue execution
@item Interrupt (pause) @tab I @tab Ctrl-G I, Ctrl-Break @tab Stop the program that runs
@item Program output @tab P @tab Ctrl-G P @tab Show program's stdout
@item debugger Options @tab O @tab Ctrl-G O @tab Select debugger backend
@item Quit @tab Q @tab Ctrl-G Q @tab Stop debugger
@end multitable

@noindent
Alternative keys (when function keys are available):
Ctrl-F8 = Toggle Breakpoint, Ctrl-F7 = Make Watch, Ctrl-F6 = Stack,
F7 = Trace, F8 = Step, Ctrl-F10 = Run, Ctrl-F2 = Quit.  In the Borland
key style, F4 in a source window is Go to cursor while a session runs,
as in Turbo C; with no session, and in Messages, Watches or Stack, it is
Search.

@cindex Go to cursor
Go to cursor works with every debugger.  gdb runs @code{advance} to
the line of the file you pressed it in, also when it is in a function
the program calls or in another source file.  The file is named as
breakpoints name it, so with two source files of the same name in
different directories gdb stops in the first one the program reaches.
pdb, jdb, a68g and the debug adapters get a breakpoint on the line that
is removed at the next stop, wherever the program stops; a breakpoint
of your own on that line stays.  Pressed before a session runs, with no
breakpoint set, Go to cursor starts the session under gdb, jdb or dlv
and runs on to the cursor's line; if the program never reaches it, the
program runs to its end, as Go to cursor does at a stop.  Under a68g
it runs as Run does, to the first breakpoint or to the end of the
program.  Under lldb-dap and gdb's DAP mode the cursor's breakpoint is
set while the program already runs, so a line the program reaches at
once can be passed and the program runs on.  The other debuggers stop
at the start of @code{main}, as Run does.  Under gdb, pdb, jdb, a68g and
the debug adapters, Go to cursor and Finish function leave the editor its
keys while the program runs, so @samp{Debug @result{} Quit} ends the
session.

@cindex Pause
@cindex Interrupt (pause)
@cindex Ctrl-Break
Pause stops a program that is running, where it is.  Press
@kbd{Ctrl-Break} (@kbd{Ctrl-Pause} on a PC keyboard) in xwpe, or
@kbd{Ctrl-G I}, or choose @samp{Debug @result{} Interrupt (pause)}, or
click @samp{^Brk Pause} in the bottom row.  The line the program was on
is marked, the Watches are refreshed, and the status row says
@samp{Paused}.  The program has no error to show for the interrupt.  A
terminal sends no Ctrl-Break that curses can read, so there use
@kbd{Ctrl-G I}, the menu or the bar.  With no session, or with the
program stopped, Pause only says so on the status row.  It works under
gdb, sdb, dbx, xdb, pdb, a68g, jdb and the debug adapters (dlv for Go,
lldb-dap and gdb's adapter for Rust).  The Scala adapter is asked to
pause like the others; whether it stops the program is up to the
adapter, and a Pause it does not answer ends at the program's next stop,
where the status row says the interrupt did not stop the program.

The debugger is sent SIGINT, as Ctrl-C does in a shell.  gdb reports
where the program was, and the green bar goes to the innermost frame
whose source you have, not to the library code the program sleeps in.
That frame is gdb's selected one, so watches on its locals show their
values, and the next F8 or F7 finishes the interrupted call and stops on
the next line of your code.
pdb stops inside its own signal handler, so the bar goes to the frame
below it, which is made pdb's current frame.  When a breakpoint is set,
pdb first stops in its own files (@file{bdb.py}, @file{pdb.py}); xwpe
sends @code{return} until pdb is out of them, so the Stack window lists
only your frames and one Continue runs the program on.  If pdb is still
in its own code after 100 of these, the status row says so.  a68g
prints the line and asks whether to terminate; xwpe answers no, and the
bar goes to that line.  A program that handles SIGINT itself is not
stopped by it: the status row says so at its next stop.
jdb is not sent a signal: it is told @code{suspend}, which stops every
thread of the Java VM and leaves jdb with no current thread.  xwpe lists
the threads, makes current the first one that runs your code, and puts
the bar on that thread's innermost frame of your code (the loop, or the
call to @code{sleep} it is in), with that frame selected for the
Watches.  Continue then sends @code{resume}, not @code{cont}, so every
thread runs again.  When the program reached a breakpoint just as Pause
went out, the bar shows the breakpoint, and the next Continue or step
also sends the @code{resume} that the @code{suspend} needs, so the
program does not stay held.  The debug adapters are sent the protocol's
@code{pause} request, and the stop that follows it is the Pause
whatever reason the adapter gives it (lldb-dap says
@samp{signal SIGSTOP}, dlv may stop inside Go's runtime): the status
row says @samp{Paused}, the bar goes to the first frame of the stack that
is in your program, and that frame is the one the Watches are read in.
Continue goes on as after any stop.  xwpe starts the debugger, and the
programs under it, with SIGINT and SIGPIPE as a shell gives them,
not ignored.  In wpe the debugger and the debug adapters run apart from
the editor's terminal, so a Ctrl-C typed during a session reaches
neither them nor the program; use Pause to stop it.  Language servers,
the assistant's commands, shell scripts run from the Run menu and the
commands of an install rule start the same way.  Language servers and
the Scala build server also run apart from the terminal, so the Ctrl-C
that stops a script leaves them running.

@cindex Show stack
@cindex Stack window
Show stack works with every debugger.  The Stack window lists the
frames innermost first, each with its function, file and line:

@example
#0  sq at p.py:2
#1  <module> at p.py:6
@end example

@noindent
@key{Return} on a frame marks that frame's line in its source window,
and the Watches window shows the variables of that frame.  gdb lists
its own @code{bt}.  pdb's @code{where}, jdb's @code{where}, a68g's
@code{calls} and the debug adapters' @code{stackTrace} are read into
the list above.  pdb's own frames before your script are left out, and
after a Pause so is pdb's interrupt handler, so the first row is the
line the program was on.  A
debug adapter also lists the language's runtime (@code{runtime.main}
for Go, the standard library for Rust) and a frame without source says
@samp{in} its file instead of @samp{at} a line.  a68g names the
procedure being run and the line of its declaration, not the line that
called it, so its outer frames show their declaration line and the main
program shows no line.

@cindex Finish function
Finish function works with every debugger.  The program runs until the
current function returns and the green bar lands in the caller.  gdb
runs @code{finish}; pdb runs @code{return} and then @code{up}, so the
bar is on the line of the call; jdb runs @code{step up}; a debug
adapter runs @code{stepOut}; a68g runs @code{finish}, which stops on
the caller's next line, the one after the call.  In the outermost frame,
@code{main} or a script's top level, there is no caller to return to:
Messages says @samp{Finish function: not meaningful in the outermost
frame.} and the session stays where it was.

@cindex breakpoints, in a running session
Breakpoints can be set and removed while a session runs, with every
debugger: gdb, pdb, jdb, a68g and the debug adapters.  A breakpoint set
with @kbd{Ctrl-G B} stops the program the next time it gets there; one
removed stops it no more; @kbd{Ctrl-G M} removes them all, and the
program then runs to its end.  Your breakpoints outlive the session:
after @kbd{Ctrl-G Q}, or a program that ran to its end, the next
@kbd{Ctrl-G R} stops on them again.

@cindex breakpoints, moved by an edit
A breakpoint moves with its line when you add or delete lines above it
(@key{Return}, @kbd{Ctrl-Y}, a paste, a block copy, move or delete, Undo,
a tool's rewrite), and in a
session the debugger is told as if you had removed it and set it on the
new line: at a stop at once, while the debugger answers a command as soon
as that answer is in, and while the program runs as for @kbd{Ctrl-G B}.
The next @kbd{Ctrl-G R} then stops on the line the margin shows.  The
line numbers are those of the edited text: until you build again they
can name different code in the program the debugger runs.

@cindex breakpoints, while the program runs
@cindex breakpoints, hollow
@cindex breakpoints, refused
A breakpoint can be set or removed while the program runs, not only at a
stop.  The margin and the breakpoint list change at once, and the
debugger is told at once too, so the program stops on a line you mark
the next time it gets there, and no longer on a line you unmark, without
a Pause first:
@itemize @bullet
@item
gdb, pdb and a68g read no command while the program runs.  xwpe
interrupts the program, as Pause does, gives the debugger the change
and lets the program run on.  Nothing of that stop is shown: no mark,
no watch refresh, no @samp{Paused}.  Several changes made in quick
succession share one interrupt, and only the margin mark that changed is
drawn again, not the screen.
@item
jdb and the debug adapters (dlv, lldb-dap, gdb's adapter, Scala) take
the change while the program runs, and xwpe sends it as soon as the keys
you have already typed are read.
@item
sdb, dbx and xdb keep the change for the program's next stop, whatever
the reason for it: a breakpoint, @kbd{Ctrl-G I}, the end of a step.
@end itemize
Changes typed together reach the debugger as their net change, under
every debugger: a mark set and taken off again in one burst of keys is
never sent, so a loop that passes the line a million times a second
cannot stop on a line you no longer mark.  A mark set and removed with a
pause between the two is sent twice; if jdb then reports a hit on the
line before it has taken the removal, xwpe does not show that stop
either, and the program runs on.  A stop on a line that still has a
mark, a step's end, an exception, or a breakpoint you never removed, is
always shown.
A change made during a step, Finish or Go to cursor waits for that
command's own stop: interrupting it would turn the step into a Continue.
Its mark stays hollow until the step ends or you press Pause, which
can be long after the change when the step is over a call that takes a
while to return.  If the program stops by itself (a breakpoint, its end)
while the interrupt is on its way, that stop is yours: it is shown, the
change is applied there, and the program does not run on until you say
so.  Pause pressed meanwhile is a Pause.  A Python script that sets its
own handler for @kbd{Ctrl-C} while it runs takes the interrupt itself
and is not stopped by it: the change is then applied at the script's
next stop, the status row says @samp{The program handles Ctrl-C itself},
and later changes in that session wait for a stop too.  Under pdb a
script inside a long call such as @code{time.sleep(6)} gets the change
at once, and the call then runs to its end.

@cindex watches, while the program runs

A watch can be made, edited, deleted, or all removed, while the program
runs.  The list changes at once and the Watches window shows
@samp{(at the next stop)} in place of the values, because the debugger
reads no command until the program stops.  The next stop that is yours
(a breakpoint, the end of a step, @kbd{Ctrl-G I}, an exception) reads every
watch as usual.  The hidden stop that applies a breakpoint set meanwhile
reads no watch and draws nothing.  If the program ends, or you quit the
session, before it stops, there is no next stop: the watches then say
what a watch says without a session (@samp{No symbol in current
context}).  With no session nothing waits.

Until the debugger has confirmed a breakpoint its line is drawn
@dfn{hollow}: the breakpoint's red as the text colour on the editor's own
background, instead of the solid red bar (underlined on a display without
colour).  It turns solid once the debugger has the breakpoint, which is
at once when you set it at a stop and, under every debugger but sdb, dbx
and xdb, while the program runs.  If the debugger's word comes while a
dialog or a menu is open, the line turns solid when it closes.

A breakpoint the debugger refuses stays in the margin, hollow, and the
status row gives the debugger's own words, as @samp{Breakpoint at line 2
not set: No source file named q.c.} (gdb, for a file the program does not
contain) or @samp{cannot set breakpoint in that line.} (a68g, for a line
with no unit); no box opens.  When the program is stopped with Pause at
the same time, these words stay on the status row in place of
@samp{Paused}: the marked line already shows the pause.  It is still your
mark, and the next start tries it again.  A breakpoint the debugger keeps
for later, as jdb does for a class it has not loaded, stays hollow too,
with the debugger's words after @samp{not confirmed}.  The debug adapters
confirm a breakpoint with their own answer, which for gdb's adapter may
come later.  With no session
every breakpoint is the solid bar.  The hollow colour is the key @code{du}
of the colour section of @file{xwperc}, written as the other keys are
(@code{du : @var{foreground} @var{background}}); a file without that key
keeps the default.  @samp{Options}, @samp{Adjust Colors}, @samp{Editor
Colors} shows it as the sample line @samp{An unconfirmed Breakpoint}.

@cindex breakpoints, in a project
With a project open, the breakpoints are saved in the project file
(@code{BREAKPOINTS=}) when the project closes or the editor quits.
Opening the project again puts them back in the margin.

@section The bottom row during a session

@cindex debugger bar
While a session runs, with any debugger or debug adapter, the bottom row
of a source window names the keys that drive it, as Turbo C's status
line did:

@example
F1 Help F7 Trace F8 Step F4 Go to cursor ^F7 Make Watch ^F2 Quit  Alt-X Quit
@end example

@noindent
While the program runs, the bar names only the keys that work then.
F7, F8 and F4 are refused until the program stops; the breakpoint and
watch keys are not, their changes wait for the stop:

@example
F1 Help  ^Brk Pause  ^F2 Quit
@end example

@noindent
Under gdb the program's output makes Messages the window you type in,
so Messages shows this bar too while the program runs.  A click on an
entry sends the key it names; a click on @samp{^Brk Pause} pauses the
program, also in a terminal that sends no Ctrl-Break.  When the session
ends, the editing keys come back.  In the Modern key style the bar names
the Modern keys: @samp{F11 Into}, @samp{F10 Over}, @samp{^F10 Go to cursor},
@samp{Shift-F5 Stop}; @key{F10} steps over only while a session is open and
opens the menu otherwise.  The Watches and Stack windows have their
own bar, where @samp{^F10 Run} continues the program.

@section Starting a session

@cindex debugger, starting
@cindex XWPE_DEBUG_START_TIMEOUT
The first Run, Step or Go to cursor builds the program and starts the
debugger (gdb, jdb, pdb or a68g).  The editor does not wait for it.
Until the debugger answers with its prompt, the last row of Messages
reads:

@example
starting gdb ... 3s  (Esc cancels)
@end example

@noindent
The editor keeps taking keys.  When the debugger answers, the command
you pressed runs.  Another debugger command pressed meanwhile does
nothing, and the status row says that the debugger is starting.

The debugger follows the file's language.  When a @file{.java} or
@file{.py} file needs another debugger than the one selected, the
status row says which one starts, for example @samp{Python file:
switching to pdb debugger.}, and the line stays there until you press
a key.  Nothing has to be dismissed.

Press @key{Esc} to end a debugger that is slow to answer.  Messages
then says @samp{gdb start cancelled}.  A debugger that is silent for
30 seconds is ended in the same way, and Messages says @samp{gdb did
not answer within 30 s}.  A debugger that closes its output while it
starts, but goes on running, can never answer either: the start keeps
waiting until you press @key{Esc} or the 30 seconds pass.  To give a
slow debugger more time, set the environment variable @env{XWPE_DEBUG_START_TIMEOUT} to a number of
seconds before you start xwpe.

@cindex XWPE_GDB_INTERFACE
gdb is driven over its line interface.  Set @env{XWPE_GDB_INTERFACE}
to @samp{mi} to drive it over its machine interface instead: the editor
then reads stops, exit codes, breakpoints and watch values from gdb's
named fields rather than its sentences, and you see the same session.
A gdb older than 7.6 keeps the line interface, and the status row says
why.

Go, Rust and Scala sessions start through a debug adapter (dlv, gdb or
lldb-dap, or the Scala build server) and start the same way.  The line
names the adapter, @samp{starting dlv ... 3s  (Esc cancels)}, and the 30
second limit applies until the adapter first answers.  After that the
adapter is known to be alive, so the limit no longer applies.  The line
then says what the adapter is doing, for example @samp{dlv building
... 40s  (Esc cancels)} while dlv builds the package, and Esc still ends
it.  If dlv cannot build the package, its error appears in Messages and
the session ends.  Rust is compiled before the adapter starts, as a
build is: Messages says @samp{Compiling for debug... (Esc cancels)},
you keep your keys, and the session starts once the compiler has
succeeded.  @key{Esc} or Debug > Quit ends the compiler, with the line
@samp{compile for debug cancelled}, and no session starts after it; nor
does one for a file closed meanwhile.  The Scala build
server is started before its adapter; that step still holds the editor.

@section While the debugger answers at a stop

@cindex debugger, waiting for an answer
@cindex XWPE_DEBUG_ANSWER_TIMEOUT
When the program stops under gdb, xwpe asks gdb where it stopped, the
value of each watch and, when the Stack window is open, the stack.  The
editor does not wait for these answers: you keep typing, moving and using
the menus while they come, and the stop is marked when gdb has answered.
If gdb is silent for a second, the status row says what it waits for:

@example
waiting for gdb to answer `where` ... 3s  (Esc cancels)
@end example

@noindent
pdb works the same way: at a stop it is asked the watches, the stack and,
after a Pause, where the script was, and a breakpoint you set at a stop
is told to it while you keep typing.  Its line names pdb and its own
command, for example:

@example
waiting for pdb to answer `p x` ... 3s  (Esc cancels)
@end example

@noindent
jdb works the same way too: at a stop it is asked the watches and the
stack, after a Pause the thread that runs your code, and a breakpoint you
set at a stop is told to it while you keep typing:

@example
waiting for jdb to answer `print x` ... 3s  (Esc cancels)
@end example

@noindent
jdb reports a breakpoint or a step's end whenever the program reaches
it, also while it answers something else (a Pause that the program's own
stop beat, for one).  xwpe never takes those words for the answer: the
watch keeps its own value, and the stop jdb reported is marked once the
answers are in.

a68g works the same way: at a stop it is asked the watches and the
stack, and a breakpoint you set at a stop is told to it while you keep
typing:

@example
waiting for a68g to answer `evaluate x` ... 3s  (Esc cancels)
@end example

@noindent
sdb, dbx and xdb work the same way: at a stop each is asked the file or
the place of the stop when its own words did not say them, the watches
and the stack, and a breakpoint you set at a stop is told to it while you
keep typing.  The line names the debugger and its own command:

@example
waiting for dbx to answer `print x` ... 3s  (Esc cancels)
@end example

A debugger command that needs gdb, such as @kbd{Ctrl-G K} or @key{Enter}
on a row of the Stack window, does nothing meanwhile and shows this line.  @kbd{F7}, @kbd{F8}, Run, Go to cursor and
Finish function are kept instead, up to eight of them, and run in the
order you typed them once gdb has answered: @kbd{F8 F8} typed while the
watches are read steps twice.  While xwpe tells gdb of a breakpoint you
set as the program runs, the program is running for you, and these keys
are refused as while it runs.

Press @key{Esc} or @kbd{Ctrl-C} to stop waiting.  The status row then
says @samp{gdb did not answer `where`: Debug > Quit ends the session},
the stop is not marked, and the keys kept for after the answer are
dropped.  An answer that has not come within 30 seconds ends the same
way, with the seconds in the line.  To give gdb more time, set the
environment variable @env{XWPE_DEBUG_ANSWER_TIMEOUT} to a number of
seconds before you start xwpe.  Debug > Quit ends the session at any
moment.

A command xwpe cannot give the debugger at all is said the same way,
naming the command and the reason: a command longer than the debugger
takes (dbx and xdb keep 255 bytes), a debugger that still owes an old
answer, a session that is over.  A breakpoint it concerns stays hollow
and the status row says @samp{Breakpoint at line 8 not set: the command
is longer than dbx takes}; the start goes on and the program runs.  When
the command is one the start needs, a box names it and the session ends.

@cindex debugger, another prompt
An answer that is another prompt of the debugger's, not the one that ends
its answers, is said within a quarter second instead of after the 30
seconds: pdb's @samp{(com)} while it reads a @code{commands} block,
@samp{((Pdb))} after @code{debug}, @samp{>>>} after @code{interact} and
@samp{...} for a statement left open; gdb's @samp{>} after @code{define},
@code{if} or @code{python}.  The status row says
@samp{pdb answered `interact` with its >>> prompt, not its own: Debug >
Quit ends the session}, and xwpe leaves the debugger there: it refuses
every command until you end the session, since anything it sent would be
read as a line of that prompt.  If the program writes a line that only
looks like such a prompt and goes on, nothing is said.

@cindex debugger, waiting while gdb starts
The same holds while gdb starts.  Once gdb has printed its banner, xwpe
gives it its settings (gdb 18 puts a symbol in front of every error and
warning, and xwpe turns those symbols off for the session, also when your
@file{~/.gdbinit} set them, so gdb's reasons read plain) and your
breakpoints, and only then runs the
command you pressed; if gdb is slow to answer, the status row shows the
same line, for example:

@example
waiting for gdb to answer `set confirm off` ... 2s  (Esc cancels)
@end example

@noindent
@kbd{F8} typed then is kept and steps once the program has stopped at
its first breakpoint.  @key{Esc} or @kbd{Ctrl-C} gives up on gdb's
answer; the next debugger key sets gdb up again, and Debug > Quit ends
the session.  Every Run, Continue, step, Finish and Go to cursor works
the same way when gdb still owes an answer before the command can go
out, such as the answer to a Pause that met a stop.  Under pdb, jdb,
a68g, sdb, dbx and xdb the start sets your breakpoints the same way, and
Go to cursor's own breakpoint and the frames Finish needs are asked before
the command goes out; under jdb, sdb, dbx and xdb so is the breakpoint on
@code{main} when you have none.
A breakpoint whose answer you gave up on is cleared first the next time,
so the line is never in the debugger twice.

@cindex debugger, not reading its input
A debugger that stops reading its input (gdb stopped from outside, an
adapter that is wedged) never stops you typing either.  A command it
does not take waits in xwpe and goes out whole, in the order you gave
it, once the debugger reads again; so does text you type for a program
that is not reading its terminal.  After 5 seconds with nothing taken
the status row says so, for example:

@example
gdb is not reading its input (3 bytes waiting): Debug > Quit ends it
@end example

@noindent
and Debug > Quit ends the session as at any other moment.

@section How a session ends

@cindex debugger, end of session
@cindex exit code
When the program ends, Messages names its exit code, as Turbo
Debugger's ``Terminated, exit code N'' did:

@example
Program exited with code 3
@end example

@noindent
gdb gives the code for every program; pdb gives it when the script
calls @code{sys.exit}, and 0 when the script runs off its end; a debug
adapter gives the code it reports when the program exits.  Under jdb the
editor reads the code from the Java VM it started: a failure code is
named as under gdb, a VM killed by a signal gives @samp{Program
terminated with signal SIGKILL}, and a program that ended normally gives
@samp{Program exited.}.  a68g reports no code, and Messages says
@samp{Program exited.}.  Under pdb
the program's output is not in Messages, so the line says where
it is: @samp{Program exited. Program output: Ctrl-G P}.  That one line
is all, with every debugger, gdb, sdb, dbx and xdb included: no box
waits for a key, not even when the editor cannot tell where the
debugger stopped, and the session ends: the green bar goes and the
editor takes keys.  A Go, Rust or Scala program that ends before it
reaches a breakpoint ends the session with its line at the first Run,
Step or Finish function.  Debug > Quit ends the
session with @samp{End of Debugging}.  A start that never became a
session names its reason only, and no end line follows it: a debugger
that cannot start because it is not installed, one you end with
@key{Esc} while it starts, and one that passes its deadline.  None of
them ran a session, so none ends one.

A debugger that ends without being asked (it crashed, or was killed)
is named in Messages and on the status row, with its exit status; a
signal counts as 128 plus its number, as in the shell.  A Go or Rust
debug adapter (dlv, lldb-dap, gdb) that goes away before the program
ends is named the same way:

@example
gdb ended unexpectedly (exit status 137)
@end example

@noindent
The session is then over, as after Debug > Quit, and the editor takes
keys again.  The line is said once, when the system reports the
debugger gone: also when a process the debugger started keeps its
output open, which used to leave the session running with no debugger
behind it.

A debugger that closes its output during a session but goes on running
can never answer again.  The session ends, the editor kills the
debugger if it is still running two seconds later, and Messages says
@samp{gdb stopped answering (its output closed)}.  The exit status the editor's own signal leaves is not
reported as the debugger's.

Debug > Quit, and the editor's own end during a session (@kbd{Alt-X}, a
logout, @code{kill PID}), tell the debugger to quit, as you would at its
prompt, so it ends its program itself; while the program runs, the
debugger cannot read that and is ended at once, its program with it.
The editor does not wait for it, and one that has not ended two seconds
later is killed.  No debugger and no
program is left behind, and the debugger is never sent a signal that
leaves a core file.

A build during a session (Compile, Make, Run, Install or Execute Make)
first asks @samp{Do You want to Quit Debugging ?}, as Turbo C asked to
reset the program before a rebuild.  Yes ends the session and builds.
No or @key{Esc} leaves the session as it was, stopped or running: the
next stop is marked and the debugger keys go on working.

After every stop, the line:column field of the window shows the line
the green bar is on.

@section Compiling for debugging

Compile with debug symbols (@code{-g}) and no optimisation
(@code{-O0}) for best results.  xwpe's default compiler options
include @code{-g}.  To add @code{-O0}, go to
@samp{Options @result{} Compiler} and append it to Compiler Options.

@section Choosing the start symbol

@cindex start symbol
@cindex constructor
@cindex breakpoint before main

By default the debugger sets its initial breakpoint at @code{main} and
runs the program up to it.  Some code runs @emph{before} @code{main} ---
for example a function marked @code{__attribute__((constructor))}, or a
C++ global object's constructor.  To stop there instead, set the start
symbol via @samp{Options @result{} Compiler @result{} start symbol} to
the name of the function you want to break at.

The example @file{docs/examples/debug_constructor.c} has a @code{setup()}
function that runs before @code{main()}.  Set the start symbol to
@code{setup}, press @kbd{Ctrl-G R}, and execution stops in @code{setup()}
before @code{main()} is reached.

@section Program output (User Screen)

@cindex User Screen
@cindex Ctrl-G P
@cindex program output
@cindex pty

Program output (e.g.@: @code{printf}, @code{System.out.println},
@code{print()}) is appended to the Messages buffer --- no screen
switching, no flicker.  For gdb, output is captured via a
pseudoterminal (pty): the program's stdout is redirected to a pty
slave and xwpe reads the pty master.  Under jdb the editor starts the
Java VM itself on that pty, so the program's output reaches Messages
the same way, as it prints.  For pdb, output is extracted from the
debugger's command pipe.

Press @kbd{Ctrl-G P} (or @samp{Debug @result{} Program output}) to
view the Messages buffer with full scroll.  The output accumulates
across steps, so you can see the complete program output at any time.

If you close Messages while the program runs, its output is still read:
under gdb, jdb, sdb, dbx and xdb what it prints while Messages is closed
goes to the User Screen (@kbd{Alt-F5}) only.  Under a68g and the debug
adapters it is kept and shown in Messages at the next stop, and all of
it is on the User Screen; of a long a68g output (past about 8 KB) only
the last part reaches Messages.

@cindex Alt-F5
A line-oriented panel cannot show a program that @emph{paints} (ANSI
colour, cursor positioning, a TUI, a progress bar).  For that, the
Borland @dfn{User Screen} on @kbd{Alt-F5} replays the program's own
full screen verbatim, then returns to the editor on any key.  Pressed
while the program runs, it shows what the program has printed so far,
without waiting for it to stop printing.  This works
in the console (@command{wpe}) and in @command{xwpe} on X11 and on
Wayland.  @command{xwpe} has no terminal to hand back, so it interprets
the program's output with an embedded terminal and paints the result in
its own window, in the colours the editor itself shows (a build without
the terminal library focuses the Messages panel instead).

@strong{Important}: the output appears only after the line that
produces it has been @emph{executed}.  If the cursor is on the
@code{printf} line, that line has not run yet.  Press @key{F8} once
more to step past it, then @kbd{Ctrl-G P} to see the output.

This needs no terminal on the editor's own input: @command{xwpe} started
from a desktop launcher debugs as well.  The program's pty is its
terminal, and the editor's own terminal is used only when no pty could
be opened.  With neither, the session is refused with the message
@samp{No terminal for the program}.

After each step, xwpe sends @code{call (void)fflush(0)} to gdb to
flush the program's stdout buffer.  This ensures output is available
immediately, even if the program uses buffered I/O.

@section Interactive programs

@cindex interactive input
@cindex stdin
@cindex Messages window input

A program that reads from standard input (@code{fgets}, @code{scanf},
@code{read}) works without any special setup: when it blocks for input,
type the answer directly in the Messages window and press @key{Enter}.
xwpe forwards your keystrokes to the program through the same
pseudoterminal it uses to capture output, so the program sees a real
terminal on stdin.  jdb works the same way: the Java program has the
pty as its terminal.  Under gdb and jdb, only text goes to the program
(characters, @key{Enter} and @key{Backspace}) while it runs; the menus,
the function keys and @kbd{Ctrl-F2} stay with the editor, so
@samp{Debug @result{} Quit} still works while the program waits.  sdb,
dbx and xdb leave the editor its keys in the same way while the program
runs, so @samp{Debug @result{} Quit} ends their session too.  So does
pdb, which gives the program no terminal of its own: text typed while it
runs goes into the file you edit, and the status row offers only
@samp{Debug @result{} Quit}.  So does a68g, which shares its input with
the program in the same way.  A
program that is running cannot be stepped, continued or asked about, so
Step, Trace, Run, Goto cursor, Finish
and Show stack say ``The program is
running'' on the status row and do nothing until it stops.  The line
stays while the program prints, until your next key or the stop.  Making,
editing, deleting or removing all watches is not refused: the list
changes at once and the values come at the next stop.  Setting or
removing a breakpoint is not refused: the debugger is told at once, or
at the next stop under sdb, dbx and xdb, the line hollow until it
confirms.  Under gdb,
jdb, sdb, dbx and xdb, @key{Enter} is text for the program from any
window, the Stack window included; under pdb and a68g it is text for the file
like the other keys.  If the
program stops while a dialog, a menu, the File Manager, the Window list
or another window that is not a source window is in front, the stop
waits.  It is shown, marked in the source, as soon as you are back in a
source window, whichever way you get there: closing the box, switching
windows, or opening a file from the File Manager.

The example @file{docs/examples/interactive_test.c} prompts for a name
and blocks on @code{fgets()}.  Press @kbd{Ctrl-F9} (Run): the prompt
appears in Messages, you type a name and press @key{Enter}, and the
program greets you with UTF-8 output.  The same works under the debugger
--- step (@key{F8}) onto the @code{fgets()} line, run it, type the
answer in Messages, and continue.

@section Debugger backends

@cindex debugger selection

xwpe supports eight backends, selectable via @samp{Debug @result{}
debugger Options} (@kbd{Ctrl-G O}).  Several are auto-selected by file
type: jdb for @file{.java}, pdb for @file{.py}, a68g for interpreted
Algol 68, and the DAP client for @file{.go} (Delve), @file{.rs} (gdb) and
@file{.scala} (Bloop via @command{scala-cli}).

When an @file{xwperc} in the folder you started in names a debugger
other than yours (@code{Debugger}), the first session of a file whose
type does not pick one asks before it starts it, in the same one box as
the rest of the start (@samp{From ./xwperc: Debugger = pdb}, with the
files that debugger would run): @samp{Run it} starts the folder's,
@samp{Use mine} yours, @samp{Cancel} none, and the answer is kept for
that folder.  A Scala project's @file{.bsp/@var{name}.json}
is asked about the same way before its build server starts
(@file{scala-cli.json} is read first, by name); only the very file and
command xwpe's own @command{scala-cli setup-ide} wrote in this session
start without a question.
@xref{Folder commands}.

@cindex debugger, start-up files
@cindex .pdbrc, .gdbinit, jdb.ini
@anchor{Debugger start-up files}
A debugger may run some files of the folder of the program you debug
on its own, before xwpe sends it anything.  When the folder holds one,
the start shows it first, with its lines, and asks @samp{Run it} or
@samp{Cancel}, with @samp{Use mine} where there is a start without it; the
answer is kept for that folder and that text, and a changed file asks
again.  A debugger that starts in the folder (all but gdb's two
interfaces, pdb and jdb) shows the file under @samp{Runs at start:}.  A line wider
than the box is wrapped, never cut.  A file the box cannot show whole
(over 16 kB, or holding a NUL byte, past which the box would show
nothing while the debugger reads on) is shown in part and not offered
to run.  The same file in your home folder is yours and is not asked
about, nor is the folder's file when the folder is your home folder.

@cindex gdb, started apart from the folder
gdb is started in an empty folder of its own, made for the session and
removed when it ends, so it finds no init file there; the editor then gives
it the program's folder (@code{cd}) and loads the program by its own
command.  Before any program is loaded the editor also sets gdb's
auto-load safe-path to gdb's own default (@code{$debugdir:$datadir/auto-load}),
whatever your @file{~/.gdbinit} set, so the folder is not a trusted source.
Your own init files still run, first; a program they load themselves is
loaded before the pin, as you chose.  Nothing of the folder's is loaded
unless the box showed it and you chose @samp{Run it}: the start then lists
the folder's @file{.gdbinit}, the script beside the program and the
scripts inside the program under @samp{With Run it:}, with their lines,
and loads exactly those files by its own commands (@code{add-auto-load-safe-path}
for the file, @code{source} for the @file{.gdbinit}), before the program is
loaded.  @samp{Use mine} (the default) starts gdb with none of them, and is
offered whenever the box opens for them.  What the box cannot list, such as the
scripts of a shared library the program loads, is never run (gdb refuses it
with a warning of its own, which xwpe does not show); the way to allow one
is a line in the folder's @file{.gdbinit}, @code{add-auto-load-safe-path
@var{file}}, which @samp{Run it} loads after the box showed it.  A
@code{set auto-load safe-path} or @code{add-auto-load-safe-path} in your
@file{~/.gdbinit} does not apply inside xwpe.  The program is loaded by its
absolute path; @code{pwd} and @code{info source} in gdb see the program's
folder.  gdb's history file, which by default is @file{.gdb_history} in the
folder gdb starts in, is moved to the program's folder.  A file your own
init files write by a relative name, such as a log, is written in gdb's own
folder, which then stays until the editor ends and goes with it; give such
a file an absolute name to keep it.  A file whose path holds a colon, a
@samp{$}, or one of @samp{*?[}, cannot be given to gdb's auto-load list, and
is not offered to @samp{Run it}; the box says so.  A path of any length the
system allows is given to gdb whole, in one command.

@cindex pdb, jdb, started apart from the folder
pdb and jdb are started the same way, in an empty folder of their own.
pdb's first input line is a @code{chdir} into the program's folder, so
the program runs there and imports the modules beside it, while a
@file{pdb.py} of the folder cannot replace the debugger.  @samp{Run it}
sends the folder's @file{.pdbrc} to pdb line by line after that move (a
line cut at @samp{;;} is sent as its commands), and sends jdb
@code{read @var{file}} for the folder's @file{jdb.ini}, else
@file{.jdbrc}; @samp{Use mine} sends neither.  Your own @file{~/.pdbrc}
and the @file{jdb.ini} in your home folder still run.  jdb reads the file
once the program's VM has connected, the moment it read it before, and
is given the program's folder as its source path.  A @file{.pdbrc}
@code{commands} block is sent line by line with its @code{end}, and runs
as pdb runs it from the file.  A line pdb would read from the terminal
(@code{interact}, @code{debug}, a statement left open) or a block the file
does not close stops the start at that line: the box names the line, the
file and the prompt, and the lines before it ran.  A file whose path
holds a blank, which jdb's one-word @code{read} cannot take, is not
offered to @samp{Run it}; the box says so.  A
@file{~/.pdbrc} that continues the program runs it before the move, in
pdb's own folder.  A folder or program path up to the system's path limit
is given to gdb, pdb and jdb whole, in one command, whatever its depth or
the letters of its names; a path that holds a control character or a
blank at its end is still refused, and one longer than the system allows
is refused with a box that says so.  A @file{.pdbrc} line is sent whole
up to the most a start-up file is read (16 thousand bytes); a longer file
is not offered to @samp{Run it}.  If the debugger refuses one of xwpe's own
commands (gdb's @code{cd}, @code{file} or an admit of a script's path; pdb's
move), the start stops and the box names the command and the debugger's
words.  A line of your own start-up file that the debugger refuses (a
@file{.pdbrc} line, a @file{.gdbinit} gdb stops reading at, a
@file{jdb.ini} jdb cannot open) is said on the status row until a key and
the start goes on, as the debugger goes on with its own start-up file.  A
@file{~/.pdbrc} that leaves pdb at another prompt before xwpe's first
command is said at the start instead of after its 30 seconds.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .30 .52
@headitem Debugger @tab File in the folder @tab When it runs it
@item pdb @tab @file{.pdbrc}
@tab Only when you choose @samp{Run it}, sent by xwpe line by line.
@samp{Use mine} loads none.
@item jdb @tab @file{jdb.ini}, else @file{.jdbrc}
@tab Only when you choose @samp{Run it}, loaded by xwpe's @code{read}.
@samp{Use mine} loads none.
@item gdb (line and MI interface) @tab @file{.gdbinit}, @file{@var{program}-gdb.gdb},
@file{-gdb.py}, @file{-gdb.scm} beside the program, and the scripts inside the program
(its @code{.debug_gdb_scripts} section, listed as
@file{./@var{program} (.debug_gdb_scripts)})
@tab Only when you choose @samp{Run it}, loaded by xwpe's own commands.
@samp{Use mine} loads none of them.
@item gdb as a Rust adapter @tab @file{.gdbinit}
@tab Only if your gdb settings allow auto-load from this folder (the
auto-load safe-path).  The box says so, and offers no @samp{Use mine} yet.
@item lldb-dap @tab @file{.lldbinit}
@tab Only if your @file{~/.lldbinit} sets @code{target.load-cwd-lldbinit}.
@item dbx @tab @file{.dbxinit} @tab Always.
@item xdb @tab @file{.xdbrc} @tab Always.
@end multitable

@noindent
sdb, a68g and dlv read nothing known from their folder.  What the
debugged program itself does is not checked: the box says @samp{This
runs the folder's own program.}  A Python program you debug still
imports the folder's own modules.

The program's @code{Arguments} go on the debugger's run command
(@samp{r @var{arguments}}), and gdb starts the program through a shell.
When the @file{xwperc} of the folder you started in sets them, the box
shows the run command they make under @samp{Then runs:} first.  A file
named like an option (@file{-command=x.c} builds @file{-command=x.e})
reaches the debugger as @file{./-command=x.e}, a program.  A value with
a line break (a project file's watch or breakpoint, Arguments, a file
name) is never written to a debugger, where the rest of the line would
be a command of its own; the status row says @samp{not sent: a watch
with a line break}.  Nor is one holding @samp{;;}, where pdb ends a
command and starts the next one.

@quotation Note
Debugging @emph{runs} your program; for IDE features that @emph{understand}
it -- completion, hover, go-to-definition and compiler diagnostics over the
Language Server Protocol (Metals for Scala) -- see @ref{Language servers}.
@end quotation

@table @strong
@item gdb (GNU Debugger)
The default backend for C, C++, Fortran, Pascal, and native (ga68)
Algol 68.  Tested with gdb 17.2.  Supports all features: breakpoints,
stepping, watches, stack, program output capture via pty.

@item DAP client (Debug Adapter Protocol)
Backend for languages whose debuggers speak DAP -- the protocol used by
VS Code, Neovim and Emacs.  Auto-selected by file type:
@itemize
@item @file{.go}: drives Delve (@command{dlv dap}).  Requires @command{dlv}
and @command{go}, and a Go module (@file{go.mod}) in the source directory,
since @command{dlv dap} builds the package.
@item @file{.rs}: xwpe compiles the file (@command{rustc -g}) and debugs the
binary with @command{gdb --interpreter=dap} -- gdb is a first-class Rust
debugger (@command{rust-gdb} is gdb).  Requires @command{rustc} and
@command{gdb}.  The adapter is chosen in @samp{Debug @result{} debugger Options}
(@kbd{Ctrl-G O}): @samp{Auto} uses the first of gdb/lldb-dap found in @env{PATH}
(so a gdb-less macOS box selects @command{lldb-dap} automatically), or pick
@samp{gdb} / @samp{lldb} explicitly.  The choice is saved in @file{~/.xwpe/xwperc}.
@env{XWPE_DAP_ADAPTER} (e.g. @code{=lldb}) overrides it at run time for scripts
and CI.
@item @file{.scala}: debugs modern Scala (3.x/2.13) through Bloop, which hosts
the Scala Center @code{scala-debug-adapter}.  The JVM has no standalone DAP
server, so xwpe drives a short Build Server Protocol (BSP) handshake against
@command{scala-cli} (which bundles Bloop) to obtain a DAP endpoint, then
connects its DAP engine to it.  Frames arrive Scala-demangled (e.g.
@code{Factorial.main(args: Array[String]): Unit}).  Requires @command{scala-cli}
(install it with coursier: @code{cs install scala-cli}); the Debian @code{scala}
package (2.11) is too old.  It is the heaviest backend at run time (a JVM build
server plus a @file{.bsp}/@file{.scala-build} project beside the source), but the
xwpe binary gains nothing heavy -- the toolchain stays an external tool.
The Scala 3 compiler crashes at start-up on a too-new JDK (24+); when the
default @code{java} on @env{PATH} is too new and an LTS JDK (17/21) is installed,
xwpe repoints @env{JAVA_HOME} at it for Bloop before starting the build server
(and likewise for Metals), so debugging works out of the box without a
@code{//> using jvm} pin.  Set @env{JAVA_HOME} yourself to override.
The handshake (initialize, the build targets, a compile, the main classes,
the debug session) runs while you keep typing: after a quiet second the
status row names the request it waits for (@samp{@dots{} build server to
answer `buildTarget/compile' @dots{} 12s (Esc cancels)}), the command you
pressed runs once the endpoint is known, and @kbd{Esc} ends the build
server's launcher and starts nothing (@samp{Scala build server start
cancelled.}).  Each request is given up after four minutes, which a first
compile that fetches the toolchain needs.
@samp{Debug @result{} Quit} ends the program Bloop runs for the session,
and so do @kbd{Alt-X}, the editor's own end on @code{SIGTERM} or a hangup,
a closed terminal, and the program running to its end; Bloop itself
is shared (Metals uses it too) and is left running.  The editor then gives
@command{scala-cli}'s launcher up to two seconds to close its connection
before ending it, while you keep typing.  An editor killed outright (@code{SIGKILL}, a crash)
cannot tell Bloop to end the program, and Bloop does not end it by itself:
look for a @command{java} process in the source's directory then and end
it with @code{kill -9}, since a program held at a breakpoint does not end
on @code{SIGTERM}.
@end itemize
Run/Continue (@kbd{Ctrl-G R}), Step Over (@kbd{F8}), Step Into (@kbd{F7}),
watches (@kbd{Ctrl-G W}, evaluated with the DAP @code{evaluate} request),
Show stack (@kbd{Ctrl-G K}, the @code{stackTrace} request), Finish function
(@kbd{Ctrl-G F}, @code{stepOut}) and Quit (@kbd{Ctrl-G Q}) all work as with
gdb.  Quit asks the adapter to end the program and waits for its answer
and its exit, up to a few seconds for an adapter that does not answer
(@command{lldb-dap} takes about a second), so no program is left
running.  An adapter that names its program (gdb does, Bloop does not)
and still leaves it running after Quit has it ended by the editor: two
seconds, then @code{SIGKILL}, to that process alone; a process the
adapter says it attached to, or one on another machine, is never ended.  The first @kbd{Ctrl-G R} stops
on your first breakpoint, even one on a line that runs only once; xwpe runs
on only past the adapter's own entry stop (Go's @code{main.main}), told
apart by the reason the adapter gives for the stop.

Run, Step, Trace, Go to cursor and Finish function wait for the adapter
as long as the program takes, the first one included, and mark the stop
whenever it comes.  The editor keeps its keys meanwhile; the debugger
keys say @samp{The program is running: Debug > Quit ends the session}, and
text typed goes into the file, since the editor gives an adapter's program
no input.  @samp{Debug @result{} Quit} ends the adapter and the program,
and the bottom row says it is waiting for them.  What the program prints
shows in Messages as it comes when Messages is open; with Messages closed it
is kept and shown at the next stop, and is on the User Screen
(@kbd{Alt-F5}).

The engine carries three transports behind one API, because each adapter
dictates its own: @emph{reverse-TCP} (Delve is a server; the debuggee runs on
a pty whose output xwpe reads), @emph{stdio} (gdb and lldb speak DAP on
their own stdin/stdout, so program output arrives as DAP @code{output}
events) and @emph{TCP-client} (connect to a DAP endpoint a build server already
started, as for Scala/Bloop).  Adding a language is a one-row descriptor;
@command{lldb-dap} for C/C++ is a planned drop-in.

@item jdb (Java Debugger)
Backend for Java.  Auto-selected for @file{.java} files.  jdb listens on
a loopback port and the editor starts the JVM on its own pty, so the
program's output reaches Messages and it reads what you type, as under
gdb.  Waits for the JVM and the program on the editor's event loop.
Tested with OpenJDK 25.

@item pdb (Python Debugger)
Backend for Python.  Auto-selected for @file{.py} files.
Breakpoints, F8 stepping, and program output capture work.
Automatically skips internal @code{<string>} frames.
Tested with Python 3.13.

@item sdb (Symbolic Debugger)
Legacy UNIX debugger.  Not available on modern Linux.

@item dbx
Berkeley UNIX debugger.  Available on some BSD systems.

@item xdb
HP-UX debugger.  Historical compatibility only.  Built only with
@option{--with-xdb}, which gives it a row of its own in the debugger
dialog beside dbx.  Without it the dialog offers six backends and dbx
keeps the row xdb would take.
@end table

@section Debugging C++ programs

gdb handles C++ natively.  xwpe selects @command{g++} as the compiler
for @file{.cpp} files and @command{gdb} as the debugger.  All debug
features work: breakpoints in member functions, stepping through
constructors, watching @code{this->member} expressions.

@section Debugging Fortran programs

gdb supports Fortran.  xwpe selects @command{gfortran} for
@file{.f90} files.  Set breakpoints on subroutine entries, step
through loops, watch local variables.  Array inspection is limited
by gdb's Fortran support.

@section Debugging Pascal programs

gdb has basic Free Pascal support.  Compile with @command{fpc -g}
(xwpe's default).  Breakpoints and stepping work.  Variable
inspection may be limited for Pascal-specific types (strings, sets).

@section Debugging Java programs

@cindex Java debugging
@cindex jdb

xwpe auto-selects jdb when opening a @file{.java} file.  No manual
configuration is needed.  If gdb was previously selected, xwpe
switches to jdb automatically and says so on the status row.

jdb differences from gdb:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{How the program starts}: jdb listens on 127.0.0.1, on a port
the system picks, and xwpe starts the JVM itself with the program's
class, telling it to connect to that port.  Nothing listens that another
user could reach to run code.  The JVM is the @command{java} next to
@command{jdb}, so a class built by that JDK's @command{javac} runs.
The VM starts in the source's folder with no class path of its own, as
@kbd{Ctrl-F9} starts it (@pxref{Compiling}), so your @env{CLASSPATH} is
honoured as @command{java} honours it: when it is set it must hold
@file{.} for the class to be found, for Run and for the debugger alike.
While jdb waits for it the waiting line in Messages says
@samp{jdb starting the Java VM}; Esc ends both.  A JVM that cannot
start says why in Messages (@samp{Could not find or load main class P},
for one) and the session ends.  Program arguments are not passed.

@item
@strong{Slower startup}: the JVM takes 2--3 seconds to initialise, and
longer for a class that works before @code{main}.  The first Run has no
deadline: the editor keeps its keys until the breakpoint is reached.

@item
@strong{A program that runs long}: Run, Step, Trace, Go to cursor and
Finish function wait for jdb as long as the program takes, and mark the
stop whenever it comes.  The editor keeps its keys meanwhile, and
@samp{Debug @result{} Quit} ends the session and the Java program at
once, also at a stop.

@item
@strong{While jdb answers}: the watches, the stack, the thread a Pause
stopped in and the breakpoints are asked while you keep typing, as under
gdb (@pxref{Debugging, While the debugger answers at a stop}):
a slow jdb shows @samp{waiting for jdb to answer `print x`} on the
status row, and @key{Esc} or @kbd{Ctrl-C} stops waiting.

@item
@strong{Uncaught exceptions}: jdb stops on the line that threw, and
xwpe marks it as it marks a breakpoint.  The status row names the
exception, also when it was thrown in a library whose source is not
there to mark.

@item
@strong{Breakpoints are deferred}: jdb sets breakpoints after the
class is loaded.  A breakpoint in a class that is not loaded yet stays
hollow, and the status row gives jdb's @samp{Deferring breakpoint} line.

@item
@strong{Program output and input}: the JVM runs on the editor's pty, as
gdb's program does.  What the program prints appears in Messages as it
prints, and what you type while it runs is its input; @key{Enter} sends
the line.  @kbd{Ctrl-G P} shows the whole output.

@item
@strong{Locale}: jdb is run in English whatever your locale, since its
stop lines are what xwpe reads; the program keeps your locale.

@item
@strong{The current line}: jdb names the class it stopped in, not a
file.  xwpe marks the line in that class's source window (@file{P.java}
for class @code{P} or @code{P$Inner}), whichever window is on top.
A class in a package whose file is not open is opened from its
package's directory, below the directory of the file you debug or the
current directory (@file{com/x/Q.java} for @code{com.x.Q}).  When no
source is found (a library class, or a second class written in another
class's file), the status row says @samp{R: source not found} the first
time and the session goes on.

@item
@strong{Watches}: @kbd{Ctrl-G W} sends @code{print @var{expr}} and
shows the value after the @samp{=}.  Every stop refreshes them.

@item
@strong{The end of the program}: jdb's ``The application exited'' is
read as the end, so Messages says @samp{Program exited.} (or
@samp{Program exited with code 3}, or the signal that killed the VM)
under the program's last output and the session ends.  The line waits
for the process the editor started to end, normally a moment after jdb's
words.  When @command{java} is a wrapper script that lives on after the
VM, the session waits for the wrapper and the line then gives its
status, as a shell would; meanwhile every key works, the debugger keys
say the program is running, and @kbd{Debug > Quit} ends the session and
the wrapper at once.

@item
@strong{Finish function}: @kbd{Ctrl-G F} sends @code{step up}.
@end itemize

Requires @command{default-jdk} (Debian/Ubuntu) or equivalent JDK
package that provides @command{javac} and @command{jdb}.

@section Debugging Python programs

@cindex Python debugging
@cindex pdb

xwpe auto-selects pdb when opening a @file{.py} file.  No manual
configuration is needed.  When another debugger was selected, the
status row says that pdb starts.

pdb differences from gdb:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{No compilation step}: @kbd{Ctrl-G R} invokes
@code{python3 -m pdb} directly on the source file.

@item
@strong{Frame skipping}: pdb starts in the @code{<string>} wrapper
frame.  xwpe automatically skips this frame so the cursor lands on
your first line of code.

@item
@strong{Program output}: captured from pdb's pipe and shown via
@kbd{Ctrl-G P} (same as gdb and jdb).

@item
@strong{Breakpoints}: set with @kbd{Ctrl-G B}, before starting pdb or
while it runs.  xwpe sends @code{break file:line} to set one and
@code{clear file:line} to remove one.

@item
@strong{Watches}: @kbd{Ctrl-G W} sends @code{p @var{expr}} and shows
pdb's answer as it is, an error included while the name is not yet
defined.  Every stop refreshes them.

@item
@strong{Finish function}: @kbd{Ctrl-G F} sends @code{return}, which
stops at the end of the function, and then @code{up}, so the green bar
is on the caller's line.  A step goes on from the end of the function.

@item
@strong{A program that runs long}: Run, Step, Trace, Go to cursor and
Finish function wait for pdb as long as the program takes, and mark the
stop whenever it comes.  The editor keeps its keys meanwhile, and
@samp{Debug @result{} Quit} ends the session.  A step that lands in
Python's own code, such as the string of an @code{exec()}, goes on by
itself, at most 100 times, to the next line of your script.

@item
@strong{While pdb answers}: the watches, the stack and the breakpoints
are asked while you keep typing, as under gdb (@pxref{Debugging, While
the debugger answers at a stop}): a slow pdb shows @samp{waiting for pdb to
answer `p x`} on the status row, and @key{Esc} or @kbd{Ctrl-C} stops
waiting.

@item
@strong{The end of the program}: a script that runs off its end says
@samp{Program exited with code 0. Program output: Ctrl-G P}; one that
calls @code{sys.exit(3)} says code 3.
@end itemize

Requires @command{python3} (Debian/Ubuntu).

@section Debugging Algol 68 programs

@cindex Algol 68 debugging
@cindex ga68
@cindex a68g
@cindex Algol 68 Genie monitor

Algol 68 has two toolchains, and xwpe debugs a @file{.a68}/@file{.alg}
file with whichever matches the file's dialect (the same content
detection used for compiling, @pxref{Compiling}).  No manual
configuration is needed; the first time you start the debugger xwpe
shows a short notice of the backend it chose (press @key{Return}).

@table @strong
@item ga68 (modern stropping)
A native compiler, so xwpe debugs the compiled binary with @strong{gdb}
-- the ordinary source-level debugger described above.  The one
wrinkle: ga68's user entry point is @code{__algol68_main}, not
@code{main}, so xwpe breaks there to stop at the start of your program
(a plain @code{break main} would land in the C runtime).  Breakpoints,
single-stepping, watches and the call stack all work as for C.

@item a68g (classic stropping)
An interpreter with a built-in, gdb-class source-level monitor
(@code{a68g --monitor}), which xwpe drives directly -- there is no
separate compile-and-link step.  Its commands differ from gdb's, as
described next.
@end table

a68g differences from gdb:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{No compilation step}: @kbd{Ctrl-G R} runs
@code{a68g --monitor} on the source file.  (Make / @key{F9} still runs
@code{a68g --norun} as a standalone syntax check.)

@item
@strong{Break at start}: a68g automatically breaks at the first line,
like gdb stopping at @code{main}.  @kbd{Ctrl-G R} then continues to your
first breakpoint (or to the end of the program).

@item
@strong{Breakpoints}: set with @kbd{Ctrl-G B}.  xwpe sends
@code{breakpoint @var{line}}.  To remove one it sends
@code{breakpoint clear breakpoints} and sets the others again, because
the monitor's @code{breakpoint @var{line} clear} leaves the line armed.
Because Algol 68 is recursive, a breakpoint inside a
procedure is re-hit on every recursive call.

@item
@strong{Watches}: @kbd{Ctrl-G W} sends @code{evaluate @var{expr}} and
shows a68g's typed value, e.g.@: @code{(INT) +5}.  The expression is
@emph{not} quoted -- type @code{n}, not @code{"n"}.

@item
@strong{Call stack}: @kbd{Ctrl-G K} sends @code{calls}.  The procedures
it names are listed innermost first, the innermost at the line the
program stopped on and the outer ones at their declaration; the main
program ends the list.

@item
@strong{Finish function}: @kbd{Ctrl-G F} sends @code{finish}.  a68g
stops on the caller's next unit, usually the line after the call.

@item
@strong{Program output}: captured from a68g's single stream and shown
via @kbd{Ctrl-G P} (Messages) and the @kbd{Alt-F5} User Screen, the same
as gdb.

@item
@strong{A program that runs long}: Run, Step, Trace, Go to cursor and
Finish function wait for a68g as long as the program takes, and mark the
stop whenever it comes.  The editor keeps its keys meanwhile, and
@samp{Debug @result{} Quit} ends the session.

@item
@strong{While a68g answers}: the watches, the stack and the breakpoints
are asked while you keep typing, as under gdb (@pxref{Debugging, While
the debugger answers at a stop}): a slow a68g shows
@samp{waiting for a68g to answer `evaluate x`} on the status row, and
@key{Esc} or @kbd{Ctrl-C} stops waiting.

@item
@strong{Program end}: a68g prints @samp{Genie finished} and exits when
the program completes; Messages says @samp{Program exited.} and the
session ends.
@end itemize

Requires @command{algol68g} (Debian/Ubuntu).

@section Languages without an integrated debugger

Not every language xwpe compiles has a debugger backend.  These compile
and run (F9 / @kbd{Ctrl-F9}) but @kbd{Ctrl-G R} has no debugger to drive:

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Perl} -- run and syntax-check only.  Use @code{perl -d}
in a terminal for interactive debugging.
@item @strong{COBOL} -- GnuCOBOL builds a native executable that gdb can
debug from the command line, but xwpe does not auto-select gdb for COBOL.
@item @strong{LaTeX} -- a document processor, not a program to step
through.
@end itemize

@section Example session: C with factorial

@example
$ wpe debug_test.c
@end example

@enumerate
@item
Move cursor to line 9 (@code{int x = 5;}).  Press @kbd{Ctrl-G B}.
The line is highlighted as a breakpoint.

@item
Press @kbd{Ctrl-G R}.  xwpe compiles with @code{gcc -c -g},
links, starts gdb, and stops at the breakpoint.

@item
Press @kbd{Ctrl-G W}, type @code{x}, @key{Return}.  Watch Window
shows @code{x = 5}.

@item
Press @key{F8} three times.  The cursor advances through:
@code{int result = factorial(x)} @result{} @code{printf(...)}
@result{} next line.

@item
Press @kbd{Ctrl-G P}.  The User Screen shows:
@example
--- Program output ---
factorial(5) = 120
--- Press any key to return to editor ---
@end example

@item
Press any key to return.  Press @kbd{Ctrl-G K} to see the stack.

@item
Press @kbd{Ctrl-G Q} to quit the debugger.
@end enumerate

@section Known issues

@itemize @bullet
@item
Debugging leaks memory.  The exact source is unknown (present since
the Payne era).

@item
The sdb, dbx and xdb backends cannot be driven against the real debuggers:
all three are proprietary, none is packaged for Linux and none has a free
implementation.  They are tested against a stub of each protocol
(@file{tests/test_legacy_debuggers.py}), which shows that xwpe sends the
commands the debugger documents and reads the answers it expects.  It says
nothing about how the real debugger behaves.  xdb is built only with
@option{--with-xdb}.

@item
Use @command{wpe} (not @command{we}) for debugging.  The editor-only
binary does not include debugger support.

@item
On keyboards where Ctrl+Function keys are not transmitted (e.g.@:
Keychron with multimedia F-keys), use the @kbd{Ctrl-G} prefix
shortcuts instead.

@item
Program output capture requires @code{libutil} (for @code{openpty}).
This is part of @code{libc6-dev} on Debian and is always available.
@end itemize

